## Rollback Runbook

This document describes how to roll back a failed deployment of **{{.Name}}**.

---

## 1. Detect — When to Roll Back

Roll back immediately if **any** of the following are true:

- [ ] Error rate on `/healthz` or key endpoints > 1% for 5+ minutes
- [ ] P99 latency has more than doubled compared to the pre-deploy baseline
- [ ] Synthetic smoke tests fail post-deploy (auto-rollback if configured)
- [ ] Database query error rate > 0.1%
- [ ] Any critical alert fires within 10 minutes of deploy
- [ ] Multiple customer-reported incidents within 5 minutes of deploy

---

## 2. Decide — Rollback Criteria

Before executing rollback, answer these questions:

| Question | Action |
|----------|--------|
| Is this code-only? (no schema changes) | Safe to roll back immediately |
| Does the deploy include a DB migration? | See §4 — DB rollback may be needed |
| Was data written in the new format? | Do NOT roll back code without reverting data first |
| Is the failure in a non-critical path? | Consider feature-flag disable instead of full rollback |

---

## 3. Execute — Rollback Steps

### Code-Only Rollback (no DB migration)

```bash
# Option A: Forge-managed rollback (M2+)
forge rollback --to <previous-release-id>

# Option B: Manual revert
git revert HEAD --no-edit
git push origin main

# Option C: Force-push previous release SHA (emergency only)
git push origin <previous-sha>:main --force-with-lease
```

### Database Migration Rollback

```bash
# 1. Check current migration state
forge migrate status

# 2. Roll back the last migration (down)
forge migrate down --steps 1

# 3. Verify schema is back to expected state
forge check schema

# 4. Then roll back the code (see above)
```

### Feature-Flag Disable (if applicable)

If the failing feature is behind a flag, disable it without deploying:

```bash
# Update the feature flag via your config system
# (Implementation-specific — document your flag system here)
```

---

## 4. Verify — Post-Rollback Checks

After executing rollback:

- [ ] `/healthz` returns `200 OK`
- [ ] `/readyz` returns `200 OK`
- [ ] Error rate returns to baseline within 5 minutes
- [ ] Run: `forge test smoke` → all pass
- [ ] Check audit log for any data written during the failed deploy: `forge audit show`
- [ ] Confirm no schema drift: `forge migrate status`

---

## 5. Postmortem — After Rollback

1. Open a postmortem issue immediately (use `forge postmortem new`)
2. Document: what failed, when, impact, timeline, root cause
3. Add a regression test that would have caught the issue
4. Update this runbook if the rollback steps were unclear or missing

